"Maximal 2-dimensional binary words of bounded degree (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.13640)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Title: Maximal 2-dimensional binary words of bounded degree ... Let d be an integer between 0 and 4, and W be a 2-dimensional word of dimensions h x w on the binary alphabet {0, 1}, where h, w in Z > 0.\nTitle: Maximal 2-dimensional binary words of bounded degree\nAuthors: Alexandre Blondin Massé, Alain Goupil, Ralphael L'Heureux, Louis Marin\nDate: Mon May 19 18:28:30 2025\n\nLet d be an integer between 0 and 4, and W be a 2-dimensional word of dimensions h x w on the binary alphabet {0, 1}, where h, w in Z > 0. Assume that each occurrence of the letter 1 in W is adjacent to at most d letters 1. We provide an exact formula for the maximum number of letters 1 that can occur in W for fixed (h, w). As a byproduct, we deduce an upper bound on the length of maximum snake polyominoes contained in a h x w rectangle.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNote that \\(\\overline{C_t}=A_t\\cup B_t\\). Since \\(|A_t|\\le \\varepsilon^{-4t}\\), we obtain (https://www.ibs.re.kr/ecopro/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Nosal.pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; A triangular edge is an edge contained in a triangle. ... *Every \\(m\\)-edge Nosal graph has at least \\(\\sqrt{m}\\) triangular edges. ... Theorem 1.2 immediately implies a weak version of Conjecture 5.1.\n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon^{-2t}m^{-1/2}\\Delta \\le \\sum_{j\\in N(i)} x_j = \\lambda x_i \\le \\lambda\\cdot \\varepsilon^{2t}.\n\\]\n\nRecalling the standard estimate \\(\\lambda \\le \\sqrt{2m}\\), we see that \\(G[C_t]\\) has max degree at most \\(\\Delta \\le \\varepsilon^{4t}m^{1/2}\\lambda \\le \\varepsilon m\\), thus it satisfies (b), as desired.\n\n5  Applications\n\nIn this section, we present some corollaries of our main results. We focus on spectral analogs of phase transitions of several graph parameters for Nosal graphs. Some of such results were known prior to our work. For example, recent results of Ning and Zhai [48, 47] show that every Nosal graph contains \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt{m})\\) triangles and \\(\\Omega(m^2)\\) copies of \\(C_4\\). In 2021, Zhai, Lin and Shu [53] proved that every Nosal graph contains a large complete bipartite subgraph \\(K_{2,r}\\) with \\(r=\\Omega(\\sqrt{m})\\). Theorem 1.2 recovers these results up to a constant.\n\n5.1  Triangular edges\n\nA triangular edge is an edge contained in a triangle. Extending Mantel’s theorem, Erdős, Faudree and Rousseau [17] proved that every \\(n\\)-vertex graph with more than \\(\\lfloor n^2/4\\rfloor\\) edges contains at least \\(2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) triangular edges. Recently, Li, Feng and Peng [29] investigated the spectral problems on triangular edges and showed a spectral version of Erdős–Faudree–Rousseau’s theorem. Moreover, they proposed the following conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 5.1** (See [29]). *Every \\(m\\)-edge Nosal graph has at least \\(\\sqrt{m}\\) triangular edges.*\n\nWe remark that if Conjecture 5.1 is true, then the bound \\(\\sqrt{m}\\) is the best possible. Similar with the graphs in Example 2.2, we consider \\(G=K_{s,t}^{+}\\), where we take \\(s=2(\\sqrt{m}+1)\\) and \\(t=\\frac12(\\sqrt{m}-1)\\). We can easily see that \\(G\\) has exactly \\(2t+1=\\sqrt{m}\\) triangular edges. Theorem 1.2 immediately implies a weak version of Conjecture 5.1.\n\n**Corollary 5.2.** *Every \\(m\\)-edge Nosal graph contains more than \\(\\frac{1}{72}\\sqrt{m}\\) triangular edges.*\n\n17--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.15071)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Title: Two problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs ... B 179 (2026) 219--249] proved that every $m$-edge Nosal graph satisfies $bk(G)> \\frac{1}{24}\\sqrt{m}$ and $τ(G) > \\frac{1}{12}\\sqrt{m}$.\nTitle: Two problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs\nAuthors: Xinghui Zhao, Lihua You, Jing Zeng, Xiaoxue Zhang\nDate: Thu Jul 16 14:43:37 2026\n\nA graph $G$ with $m$ edges is said to be a Nosal graph if $ρ(G)>\\sqrt{m}$. For a graph $G$, we write $bk(G)$ for its maximum book size and $τ(G)$ for the number of edges contained in triangles. Li, Liu and Zhang [J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 179 (2026) 219--249] proved that every $m$-edge Nosal graph satisfies $bk(G)> \\frac{1}{24}\\sqrt{m}$ and $τ(G) > \\frac{1}{12}\\sqrt{m}$. Recently, Zhai, Li and Lou [arXiv:2601.10163v2] proved that every $m$-edge Nosal graph satisfies $bk(G)> \\frac{1}{9}\\sqrt{m}$. In this paper, we establish the following result: Every $m$-edge graph $G$ with no isolated vertices and $ρ(G)\\geq \\sqrt{m}$ that is not isomorphic to any complete bipartite graph satisfies $bk(G)\\geq\\frac{ρ(G)}{3}$ and $τ(G)\\geq ρ(G)$. As direct consequences, we answer a question of Li, Liu and Zhang [J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 179 (2026) 219--249] and confirm a conjecture of Li, Feng and Peng [J. Graph Theory 110 (4) (2025) 408--425].--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReal symmetric matrices and their negative eigenvalues (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/357840248_Real_symmetric_matrices_and_their_negative_eigenvalues)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Real symmetric matrices and their negative eigenvalues ... Ali Mohammadian ... Image: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\n# Real symmetric matrices and their negative eigenvalues\n\n  * January 2022\n  * Linear Algebra and its Applications 640(59)\n\nDOI:10.1016/j.laa.2022.01.011\n\nAuthors:\n\nAli Mohammadian\n\nAli Mohammadian\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor two integers k≧0 and q≧1, consider symmetric matrices M with k negative eigenvalues counted with multiplicities and q pairwise distinct values of entries such that the rows of M are mutually distinct and the largest diagonal entry of M is less than or equal to the smallest off-diagonal entry of M. It is shown that the number of such matrices is finite when k and q are fixed. This generalizes some known results on the adjacency matrices of graphs. It is conjectured that any twin-free graph on n vertices with no isolated vertices has at least −1+log2⁡(n+2) negative adjacency eigenvalues.\n\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest full-text PDF--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2607.15071] Two problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs (https://www.tempodimare.com/?_=%2Fabs%2F2607.15071%23nIKhFiPUZajrdh0GkGynKZM%3D)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last month; View a PDF of the paper titled Two problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs, by Xinghui Zhao and 3 other authors ... B 179 (2026) 219--249] proved that every $m$-edge Nosal graph satisfies $bk(G)> \\frac{1}{24}\\sqrt{m}$ and $\\tau(G) > \\frac{1}{12}\\sqrt{m}$.\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 16 Jul 2026 (v1), last revised 21 Jul 2026 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:Two problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs\n\nAuthors:Xinghui Zhao, Lihua You, Jing Zeng, Xiaoxue Zhang\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Two problems on booksize and triangular edges in Nosal graphs, by Xinghui Zhao and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:A graph $G$ with $m$ edges is said to be a Nosal graph if $\\rho(G)>\\sqrt{m}$. For a graph $G$, we write $bk(G)$ for its maximum book size and $\\tau(G)$ for the number of edges contained in triangles. Li, Liu and Zhang [J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 179 (2026) 219--249] proved that every $m$-edge Nosal graph satisfies $bk(G)> \\frac{1}{24}\\sqrt{m}$ and $\\tau(G) > \\frac{1}{12}\\sqrt{m}$. Recently, two results on the booksize constant are proved: $\\frac{1}{9}$ by Zhai, Li and Lou [arXiv:2601.10163v2], and $\\frac{1}{4}$ by Chen, Li and Tang [arXiv:2607.16746v1].\n> In this paper, we establish the following result: Every $m$-edge graph $G$ with no isolated vertices and $\\rho(G)\\geq \\sqrt{m}$ that is not isomorphic to any complete bipartite graph satisfies $bk(G)\\geq\\frac{\\rho(G)}{3}$ and $\\tau(G)\\geq \\rho(G)$. As direct consequences, we answer a question of Li, Liu and Zhang [J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 179 (2026) 219--249] and confirm a conjecture of Li, Feng and Peng [J. Graph Theory 110 (4) (2025) 408--425].\n\nComments:  | 18 pages\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2607.15071 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2607.15071v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.15071 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Xinghui Zhao [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 16 Jul 2026 14:43:37 UTC (15 KB)\n[v2] Tue, 21 Jul 2026 02:40:01 UTC (16 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReal symmetric matrices and their negative eigenvalues - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Publication%3A2668061)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Real symmetric matrices and their negative eigenvalues ... Mohammadian ... negative eigenvalue Ramsey number symmetric matrix twin-free graph\n# Real symmetric matrices and their negative eigenvalues\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nPublication:2668061\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1016/J.LAA.2022.01.011MaRDI QIDQ2668061zbMATH OpenOpenAlexWikidataFDO\n\nAuthors A. Mohammadian\n\nPublication date 3 March 2022\n\nPublished in Linear Algebra and its Applications (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://doi.org/10.1016/j.laa.2022.01.011\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\